Hi Jagannath,

Thanks for the thoughtful questions. Let me go through them, and I've tried to be
precise about where data lives and how it is controlled at each step.

1) LAB vs PROD scope. Both agents operate in the LAB scope. The detection and
root-cause agent works on the EVE-NG digital twin, which is a LAB copy generated
from production configuration, not the production system itself; the agent reads the
live twin read-only and only experiments in a sandboxed copy, never touching
production. The prediction work uses historical NUAR data, but training and
validation are done offline on the LAB side. So to answer directly: the input data
for both agents is LAB scope.

2) Anonymisation. The digital twin is itself a structural abstraction: it reproduces
the network's topology, routing and configuration logic and its load patterns, not
the customer traffic it carries. The agents reason over operational metadata
(link utilisation, IS-IS/BGP state, configuration diffs), which contains no user
payload and no personal data. Customer reachability is represented by prefix
identifiers, not customer identities. On top of that, I can pseudonymise the
identifiers (device names, prefixes mapped to stable pseudonyms) before anything is
placed in an LLM context, reusing the inventory-mapping layer we already have.

3) Synthetic NUAR data. It is machine-generated, produced by the twin's traffic
model and fault injector, deterministic and reproducible from a fixed seed. It is not
derived from real production traces; it provides structurally realistic but
content-synthetic data for training and testing. Where real NUAR data is later used
for validation, it stays on the LAB side.

4) LLM APIs and data-export control. This is the most important point, so let me be
explicit. The system runs OFFLINE BY DEFAULT and requires no external API: the core
detection, the rule-based root-cause reasoning and the evaluation all run locally
with zero external calls. The LLM is an OPTIONAL enhancement behind a clean
interface. When an LLM is used, the intended deployment is an internally hosted or
private in-VPC endpoint, not a public chat interface, so there is no contact with
public forums and no use of the data for training. And even against such an endpoint,
what is sent is pseudonymised operational metadata, not raw production configuration,
with an outbound-filtering/audit step. If security policy requires full localisation,
the default form of the system already satisfies it; the deployment mode of the
optional LLM layer is yours to decide.

Happy to walk through the data-flow diagram in more detail if useful, and to align
the design with whatever the security guidelines require.

